// @check
// @error-at 31:13 'area' is not imported — a free `area(self: …)` accepting Box is exported by "./lib"; a UFCS call resolves only names in scope, so import `area` from there — add `area` to the existing `import { … } from "./lib"`
// @error-at 37:8 no field 'nosuch' on Box
// @error-at 42:8 no field 'hidden' on Box
// @error-at 47:39 'area' is not imported — a free `area(self: …)` accepting Box is exported by "./lib"; a UFCS call resolves only names in scope, so import `area` from there — add `area` to the existing `import { … } from "./lib"`
// D1230 — THE MESSAGE NAMES THE MISSING IMPORT, and the program stays illegal.
//
// UFCS is EXPLICIT by ruling (DECISIONS.md §"UFCS is never implicit"): `b.area()`
// resolves `area` only against names in scope, and the compiler does not look
// into the module that defines `Box`. So this file does not compile and must not
// — what changed is the SENTENCE. `no field 'area' on Box` is true, permanent,
// and useless: `Box` has no such field and never will, while the compiler knew
// all along that `area` is a free `self`-function in a module this very file
// imports from.
//
// The diagnostic also carries the bare code `ufcs-not-imported`, with the member
// name, the module specifier(s) and the receiver type on the parallel DATA
// channel, so an editor's quick-fix keys on the code and never parses these words
// (`tests/selfhost_native_diag_code_test.ts` pins the payload; this file pins the
// sentence).
//
// THE TWO CONTROLS ARE THE OTHER HALF OF THIS FILE, and both live in the SAME
// module graph as the suggestion — so neither can pass merely because the
// registry is empty:
//
//   * `nosuch` names no self-function anywhere — the old sentence survives.
//   * `hidden` IS a self-function in `./lib` taking exactly this receiver, and is
//     NOT exported — the old sentence survives, because there is no import that
//     would make it resolve.
import { Box, box, bump } from "./lib"

print(box(5).area())

// A name that is not a field, not a method, and not a free self-function in any
// module of the graph. Nothing to suggest, so nothing is suggested — this is the
// control that proves the enrichment did not widen into every missing member.
const b = box(2)
print(b.nosuch())

// A self-function that exists, in a module the entry imports, taking exactly this
// receiver — and un-exported. An import naming it would be refused, so the
// suggestion must not appear.
print(b.hidden())

// A SECOND DELIVERY POSITION for the same suggestion: the receiver is a declared
// PARAMETER rather than a call result, so the sentence is not riding on how this
// one expression happened to be typed.
function useIt(z: Box): i32 { return z.area() }
print(useIt(box(3)))

print(bump(b))
